<!--/Post: 11277-->
<!--Post: 11310--><!--Time: 1110571511-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11310"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Iceteajan<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Iceteajan<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, March 11, 2005 - 12:05 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Embersage, <BR> <BR>I&#39;m a bit confused about obtaining DNA profiles from medical records. <BR> <BR>Since when are DNA profiles kept in your medical file?  I mean blood types may be in there, but DNA profiles? <BR> <BR>Help me understand this.   <BR> <BR>I mean . . . she just happened to give a sample to some lab or doctor at the SAME VERY TIME the FBI needed it? <BR> <BR>I find that a weird coincidence, UNLESS they were laying in wait for her annual &#34;check-up&#34; and then moved in at that point?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11310-->
<!--Post: 11311--><!--Time: 1110576631-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11311"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Foxfire<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Foxfire<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, March 11, 2005 - 1:30 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Didn&#39;t I read that it&#39;s part of the new Patriot Act that they can use a warrant to obtain anyone&#39;s medical file for DNA purposes if it involves a possible criminal offense? <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11311-->
<!--Post: 11327--><!--Time: 1110590245-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11327"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:embersageblue@aol.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Embersage<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Embersage<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, March 11, 2005 - 5:17 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Anyone who has had blood taken is subject to a DNA profile, that is all they need a few drops of your blood. And I will look up the Patriot Act ammendments, I do believe you are correct. The government has created a lot of new laws to protect the country from Terrorist, please don&#39;t ask for my opinion on that  Thanks E.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11327-->
<!--Post: 11333--><!--Time: 1110591407-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11333"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:williammichael@direcway.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Oklahoma_mike<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Oklahoma_mike<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->11-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, March 11, 2005 - 5:36 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->If anyone has had a medical biopsy done, even if it is on the removal of a suspicious mole on the skin, the DNA profile of that sample is usually kept in the patient&#39;s medical record for future comparison.  If Kerri Rader had a possible skin cancer removed &#40;for example&#41; 10 years ago her Dr. and/or the lab would keep the results.  This is to compare with any future samples taken to see if amy cell mutation &#40;cancer&#41; has occurred. <BR>The possible types of procedures she might have had which would have resulted in keeping of the sample are numerous and no need to discuss each. <BR>As to the question of legality of search of medical records, where do you think you are, America?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11333-->
<!--Post: 11340--><!--Time: 1110594103-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11340"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:embersageblue@aol.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Embersage<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Embersage<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, March 11, 2005 - 6:21 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text--><a href="http://www.bambooweb.com/articles/u/s/USA_Patriot_Act.html" target=_top>http://www.bambooweb.com/articles/u/s/USA_Patriot_ Act.html</a> <BR>Here is the link to the Patriot Act, what i saw applied to the Dna of terrorists, I may have missed it. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11340-->
<!--Post: 11350--><!--Time: 1110604579-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11350"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Foxfire<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Foxfire<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, March 11, 2005 - 9:16 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Well, I agree that what I think I understans is that these laws only apply to foreign terrorists or possibly citizens talking to or aiding foreign terrorists. <BR> <BR>So that brings us right back to where we started on learning just how that all played out concerning how they obtained Rader&#39;s daughter&#39;s DNA. <BR> <BR>Maybe it will all become more clear later on as to exactly how it was obtained.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11350-->
<!--Post: 11362--><!--Time: 1110608916-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11362"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:embersageblue@aol.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Embersage<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Embersage<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, March 11, 2005 - 10:28 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I doubt it I think it was a screw up, because of the first mistake, LE wanted to be sure this time, What would happen it the Lawyers said they  obtained the daughter&#39;s DNA without cause, Boy that would cause a few heads to roll, Then The DNa evidence would be thrown out. We have a Lawyer on this Board where&#39;s she at?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11362-->
<!--Post: 11378--><!--Time: 1110645031-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11378"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Teardrop<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Teardrop<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, March 12, 2005 - 8:30 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Perhaps they obtained a pap test slide. I believe that those are kept for awhile &#40;CYA and lawsuits&#41;. Someone a few years ago was trying to auction off a slide belonging to Marilyn M. Who knows what stealth infomation is out there on all of us. It is just &#39;good data collecting&#39; by the govt to do this. Personal privacy rights have been gradually eroded away.  Many years ago I went to a new doctor. He ran the traditional blood profile that one would run on a patient and they told me what they would do. When I spoke with him about the results, he apologized and told me that they also ran a VDRL &#40;I believe those are the right initials&#41; for VD and gonorrhea. I am sure I paid for that as well. He stated that they ran it on everyone. My father was a health inspector and I would look at the papers in the health dept office. Statistics and other things. I am sure that all those test results were sent to the state office for inclusion in those stats. Do not be so innocent in your thinking as to exclude a massive DNA data base being gathered by the Feds. I do not trust the Feds as our govt is no longer of, by, and for the people. The judiciary is creating a huge number of laws with no way to police or control them. Too many coverups, lies, videotapes, and disinformation. We can all rightly be paranoid.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11378-->
<!--Post: 11392--><!--Time: 1110664237-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11392"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Weeza<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Weeza<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, March 12, 2005 - 1:50 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->just a minute here...didn&#39;t they ask his daughter directly for a blood sample, as I was told, so that her father could be eliminated as a suspect, and that is where the DNA match came from? Geez, pap smears? Its easier to go directly to the source, which is what has been reported. His name was found on a computer disk, they tracked his daughter down and asked for a voluntary test.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11392-->
<!--Post: 11408--><!--Time: 1110675776-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11408"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:embersageblue@aol.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Embersage<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Embersage<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, March 12, 2005 - 5:02 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->no they did not ask her, probably didn&#39;t want to tip Rader off. They took it from her medical records. Arrested RAder then did the DNA test while he was been interigated by LE that first day.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11408-->
<!--Post: 11438--><!--Time: 1110721702-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11438"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Victoria<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Victoria<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, March 13, 2005 - 5:48 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->She gave it, she didn&#39;t, they took it. Come on people! If she willingly gave it, it looks bad. If they took it without her knowledge, many of U.S. citizens will not like it, regardless of who got caught because of it. <BR>  The daughter is just too young for all that medical stuff. It will all come out in court when they figure how best to present it to the public. <BR>  Did the police ask for a sample and she didn&#39;t tell her father? Well, that is also quite suspicious. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11438-->
<!--Post: 11440--><!--Time: 1110729885-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11440"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Sidewalker<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Sidewalker<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, March 13, 2005 - 8:04 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I have read several different scenarios regarding the dna. <BR>I dont know which is true. <BR>Im not really comfortable with LE being able to get my medical info from my doctors/lab, but apparently they are able to, if they get a warrant. They wouldnt be able to get a warrant without some pretty good evidence that I had committed a crime.  <BR>I think LE had Radars car info from a video at home depot, and that floppy disk from the church. I imagine at some point we will know how this all played out. But not now. <BR> <BR>I dont think its at all suspect that the daughter would have provided a dna sample to LE.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11440-->
<!--Post: 11444--><!--Time: 1110735410-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11444"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:williammichael@direcway.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Oklahoma_mike<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Oklahoma_mike<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->11-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, March 13, 2005 - 9:36 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Check back on all the news articles and I believe the order of events, and reasons went like this: <BR> <BR>1.Police had decided Dennis Rader was a prime suspect. <BR>2.Police obtained a search warrant for his families medical records in search of any dna. <BR>Daughters dna was on file from some previous procedure.  It could have been any number of things, a suspected skin cancer, a breast lump, any type of tumor.  Such biopsy tissue results are kept on file with a Dr. in case of future problems whcih might need comparison. <BR>3.The daughter&#39;s dna indicated 90% chance her father was a match. <BR>4.Daughter was asked, at home in Michigan, for a current dna sample, told to clear a family member.This would be done for evidence sake, to make sure the dna in the older medical sample was the same as the sample taken directly from her. <BR>5.Dennis Rader arrested, dna sample taken. <BR>6.Daughter&#39;s 2 samples &#40;old medical, new swab&#41; matched. <BR>7.Dennis Rader&#39;s dna was 100% direct match. <BR>Bingo. <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11444-->
<!--Post: 11445--><!--Time: 1110736638-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11445"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Teardrop<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Teardrop<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, March 13, 2005 - 9:57 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I cannot imagine that the daughter could keep quiet about all of this while the &#39;study&#39; was being done on her father. I would want to call my mother and say &#39;run for your life&#39;! Kerri would surely have awakened screaming.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11445-->
<!--Post: 11446--><!--Time: 1110741003-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11446"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Victoria<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Victoria<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, March 13, 2005 - 11:10 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I know all about the Unibomber and his brother but I just want to take a poll here: <BR> <BR>1-Would you give a DNA sample if the police asked for it to clear your SON of a murder &#40;and not tell him&#41;? <BR> <BR>2-Would you give a DNA sample if the police asked for it to clear your FATHER of a murder &#40;and not tell him&#41;? <BR> <BR>3-Would you give a DNA sample if the police asked for it to clear your MOTHER of a murder &#40;and not tell her&#41;?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11446-->
<!--Post: 11451--><!--Time: 1110755158-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11451"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:embersageblue@aol.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Embersage<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Embersage<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, March 13, 2005 - 3:05 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->No way to all three I could never sit here that long and wait i would be calling them asap, to say hey guess what<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11451-->
<!--Post: 11464--><!--Time: 1110768983-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11464"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Johno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Johno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, March 13, 2005 - 6:56 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Let&#39;s put his to rest once and for all. <BR>Oklahoma Mike is mostly correct in his chronology of events #1-3 and #5-7. Let me eloborate on #4. <BR>The asking of 26 year old Kerri Rader to provide a DNA sample was done in a frame of time to coordinate with the arrest of her father Dennis Rader. She provided the DNA as police were already at Dennis Rader&#39;s home conducting the search that led to his arrest. <BR>Farmington Hills, Michigan police chief Charles Nebos was contacted by FBI agents in Michigan to alert him to the fact that agents were at the apartment of Kerri Rader. This call was made 45 minutes after police first arrived at Dennis Rader&#39;s home. At the same time that police conducted the search of Dennis Rader&#39;s home in Wichita, Kansas, the FBI agents in Michigan simultaneouly arrived at Kerri Rader&#39;s home in Farmington Hills, Michigan. Had she called her parents home when the FBI arrived she would have learned that FBI agents were there also. <BR>Agents asked for her DNA sample to eliminated her father as a matter of routine procedure in their investigation. She did. It matched the DNA sample of BTK and so did Dennis Rader&#39;s.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11464-->
<!--Post: 11485--><!--Time: 1110781299-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11485"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, March 13, 2005 - 10:21 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->How would you know this Johno?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11485-->
<!--Post: 11513--><!--Time: 1110820146-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11513"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lctrip<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lctrip<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, March 14, 2005 - 9:09 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Oklahoma Mike and Johno have got the timeline down.  The initial match came from her medical files and Kerri Rader had no knowledge of that.  The second sample was taken after Rader had been taken into custody and the guise used with Ms. Rader was that it was needed to eliminate her father as a suspect. <BR> <BR>Rocky, the information came out in the news articles after Rader&#39;s arrest.  There was a lot of initial speculation that Ms. Rader had turned her father in.  Over the course of several days the story was gradually &#39;clarified&#39; by law enforcement to the timeline/version that OK Mike and Johno detailed.  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11513-->
<!--Post: 11550--><!--Time: 1110904299-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11550"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Freya<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Freya<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, March 15, 2005 - 8:31 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Weeza, I agree, the idea of the FBI eagerly waiting for a woman&#39;s pelvic exam is just, well, creepy. <BR> <BR>I thought all along that she gave her DNA knowing that her father might be a suspect. Thanks, all, for enlightening me. That&#39;s what I get for listening to the media!<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11550-->
<!--Post: 11700--><!--Time: 1111034393-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11700"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Johno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Johno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, March 16, 2005 - 8:39 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I should add that in the synchronized visits to Dennis Rader&#39;s home in Park City, Kansas and his daughter Kerri&#39;s home in Farmington, Michigan a third investigative team was at the same time in Connecticut at the Groton Naval Submarine Base Basic Submarine School to question Seaman Apprentice Brian Rader. No word on if DNA samples were asked of Brian Rader but his questioning did correspond with the time frame of the investigations in Kansas and Michigan. Rader has been recieving counseling from Naval Chaplins.  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11700-->
<!--Post: 11702--><!--Time: 1111034918-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11702"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, March 16, 2005 - 8:48 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I knew this LC and Freya, but that is not what Johno is suggesting. He clearly states, that agents asked for her DNA sample. I knew her DNA sample came from a former doctor in Wichita.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11702-->
<!--Post: 11769--><!--Time: 1111151663-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11769"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:embersageblue@aol.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Embersage<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Embersage<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, March 18, 2005 - 5:14 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Mail From Kiehl <BR>Embersage: <BR> <BR>Access to medical records is controlled by the Health Insurance Portability <BR>and Accounting Act of 1996 &#40;HIPAA&#41;, which requires a protective order and <BR>notice to the patient before those records can be disclosed.  The notice <BR>requirement allows the patient an opportunity to object. <BR><a href="http://www.hhs.gov/ocr/privacysummary.pdf" target=_top>http://www.hhs.gov/ocr/privacysummary.pdf</a> <BR> <BR>I don’t think the DA had a protective order, but they didn’t give his <BR>daughter notice. <BR> <BR>Without her DNA there is no probable cause for their search and arrest <BR>warrants which would throw out the confessions too. <BR> <BR>thank you K.,    E. <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11769-->
<!--Post: 11785--><!--Time: 1111173999-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11785"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:bmodule_244@hotmail.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Bmodule<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bmodule<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, March 18, 2005 - 11:26 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->The local news last night called into question whether the proper proceedure was used to obtain the DNA records. Specifically whether the warrant was issued by the Court i.e. a Judge, or by the District Attorney&#39;s Office.  <BR> <BR>A law proffessor a WSU said that, if the wrong type of warrant was used, then the DNA evidence and the subsequent evidence obtained in the search of the Rader property, which was obtained by the rule of probable cause based, at least partially, on the DNA evidence could possibly be ruled inadmissable in court.  <BR> <BR>So far, This is all in the speculative stage, probably because of the computer disk. But if the DNA evidence was named, solely, as probable cause for the search warrants presented to search the property, any evidence found would be of no use in court. If used solely on the arrest warrant, Then any confessions could be compromised if not tossed also. <BR> <BR>The Wichita Eagle said no requests for warrants are on public record to the Court, and that the only way that Kerri&#39;s DNA records could be legally obtained without such a warrant would be if she had agreed, in advance, to provide it &#40;which of course the police have publicly denied&#41;. <BR> <BR>So...conceivably this case could be tossed out of court on a technicality. So...If anyone has got a good lead on subsequent victims that would constitute new charges against Rader...or any new evidence for that matter, now would be a good time to start posting. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11785-->
<!--Post: 11926--><!--Time: 1111427241-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11926"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lctrip<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lctrip<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, March 21, 2005 - 9:47 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Bmodule - wow!  That&#39;s some great information. <BR> <BR>What REALLY makes it interesting &#40;for me at least&#41; is that Rader has reportedly confessed to the 10 murders and there were murmurings that a plea was in process.  The confession was within a couple of days of Rader being taken into custody. <BR> <BR>Now we&#39;re learning that the whole body of evidence seized on and after 2/25 may be suspect because of improper procedures!  That would be just what we&#39;d expect from the Keystone &#40;oops, Wichita&#41; Kops.  Bet Rader wishes he knew about that little development before he started to sing. <BR> <BR>Although they still have all the pre 2/25 communications - perhaps there will be a way to tie Rader to the crimes with that. <BR> <BR>If the wrong type of warrant reports are true I bet you the folks in LE are getting even less sleep now than they were before Rader was apprehended.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11926-->
<!--Post: 12415--><!--Time: 1112200744-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12415"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Teardrop<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Teardrop<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, March 30, 2005 - 8:39 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I read that the tissue sample used was from a pap smear. Those have to be archived, so to speak, because of liability. CYA. The following site has lots of info and good timelines. <BR> <BR><a href="http://64.233.187.104/search?q=cache:qvfdbcCtXucJ:handwritinguniversity.com/crime/btkkiller/btk6.html%2BKerri%2BRader&amp;hl=en" target=_top>http://64.233.187.104/search?q=cache:qvfdbcCtXucJ: handwritinguniversity.com/crime/btkkiller/btk6.htm l&#43;Kerri&#43;Rader&amp;hl=en</a><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12415-->
<!--Post: 12442--><!--Time: 1112230875-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12442"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Phoenix<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Phoenix<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->3-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, March 30, 2005 - 5:01 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->bmodule: <BR> <BR>This may be off topic, apologies if it is, but the computer disk apparently either belonged to the church or was used by DR in his work at the church. <BR> <BR>If he was writing to the pastor as a church member, the priest-penitant priviledge might exclude that evidence. <BR> <BR>Here&#39;s a link to the statute: <a href="http://www.kslegislature.org/legsrv-statutes/getStatuteFile.do?number=/60-429.html" target=_top>http://www.kslegislature.org/legsrv-statutes/getSt atuteFile.do?number=/60-429.html</a> <BR> <BR>A penitent is &#34;&#34;penitent&#34; means a person who recognizes the existence and the authority of God and who seeks or receives from a regular or duly ordained minister of religion advice or assistance in determining or discharging his or her moral obligations, or in obtaining God&#39;s mercy or forgiveness for past culpable conduct&#34; <BR> <BR>To qualify as a privileged communication: &#34;penitential communication&#34; means any communication between a penitent and a regular or duly ordained minister of religion which the penitent intends shall be kept secret and confidential and which pertains to advice or assistance in determining or discharging the penitent&#39;s moral obligations, or to obtaining God&#39;s mercy or forgiveness for past culpable conduct.  <BR> <BR>If DR was writing the pastor for moral advice, it&#39;s an issue.   <BR> <BR>Hopefully it&#39;s just a laundry list. <BR> <BR>Phoenix <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12442-->
<!--Post: 12460--><!--Time: 1112254952-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12460"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lostdog<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lostdog<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, March 30, 2005 - 11:42 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Phoenix, <BR> <BR>On or off topic, that IS something to chew on. <BR> <BR>As I understand it, Rader included the disc as part of a communication directed to the news media and/ or law enforcement. <BR> <BR>Do the protections provided for in the statute extend to “confessions”, or remnants thereof, that are willfully or carelessly distributed to third parties by the penitent? <BR> <BR>Rader’s actions sure seem to negate any intent to maintain a confidence with Pastor Mike. <BR> <BR>_ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _  <BR> <BR> <BR>Given what is known about the “Death to Landwehr” communiqué, why was Rader not charged with making terrorist threats? <BR> <BR>I haven’t read the entire Patriot Act – but excerpts lead me to believe that, had they wanted to, federal agents could have cut off Kerri Rader’s left hand to obtain a DNA sample... <BR> <BR>O.K… maybe just a finger. <BR> <BR>What do you think P’x? <BR> <BR> <BR> <BR>“Warrant?.. We don’t need no stinking warrant! <BR> <BR>FredC.Doggs <BR> <BR> <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
