<!--Post: 12483--><!--Time: 1112292340-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12483"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Phoenix<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Phoenix<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->3-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, March 31, 2005 - 10:05 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->l&#39;dog <BR> <BR>Here&#39;s a link to the federal site on HIPPA, this link goes to a FAQ on law-enforcement access to medical records, saying that HIPPA restricts access more than prior state law. <a href="http://answers.hhs.gov/cgi-bin/hhs.cfg/php/enduser/std_adp.php?p_sid=moNzSNBh&amp;p_lva=&amp;p_faqid=349&amp;p_created=1040407596&amp;p_sp=cF9zcmNoPTEmcF9ncmlkc29ydD0mcF9yb3dfY250PTYzJnBfc2VhcmNoX3RleHQ9bGF3IGVuZm9yY2VtZW50JnBfY2F0X2x2bDE9NyZwX2NhdF9sdmwyPX5hbnl_JnBfcGFnZT0x&amp;p_li=" target=_top>http://answers.hhs.gov/cgi-bin/hhs.cfg/php/enduser /std_adp.php?p_sid=moNzSNBh&amp;p_lva=&amp;p_faqid=349&amp;p_c reated=1040407596&amp;p_sp=cF9zcmNoPTEmcF9ncmlkc29ydD0 mcF9yb3dfY250PTYzJnBfc2VhcmNoX3RleHQ9bGF3IGVuZm9yY 2VtZW50JnBfY2F0X2x2bDE9NyZwX2NhdF9sdmwyPX5hbnl_JnB fcGFnZT0x&amp;p_li=</a> <BR> <BR>Phoenix <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12483-->
<!--Post: 12534--><!--Time: 1112393548-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12534"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Foxfire<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Foxfire<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 01, 2005 - 2:12 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->&#34;Nightmare in Wichita&#34; reports that the police did get a court order for Kerri&#39;s medical records.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12534-->
<!--Post: 12573--><!--Time: 1112473467-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12573"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Phoenix<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Phoenix<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->3-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, April 02, 2005 - 12:24 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->If they did have a “court” order, they probably complied with HIPPA. <BR> <BR>Problem is that there are no orders on file with the court clerk.  While a Judge can &#34;seal&#34; a court record to keep its contents private, it still gets filed, and the existence of the record is a public fact.  <BR> <BR>A Kansas District Attorney can investigate any crime where the defendant is a &#34;fugitive from justice&#34; and issue a subpoena without court approval. Otherwise the DA files a request to commence an &#34;Inquisition&#34; and a request for a subpoena.  Then the court issues the subpoena, it gets served, and a &#34;return&#34; is filed with the court.  There is no court record if the DA issues it; there is a record if the court does it.   <BR> <BR>Because there is no court record of a subpoena, it is probably a DA&#39;s subpoena, which would not comply with HIPPA. <BR> <BR>Phoenix <BR> <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
